nodecode-file-lineage is a rewrite of P0GL0L's P0GL0L/hermes-lineage:

  https://github.com/P0GL0L/hermes-lineage
  commit 5d9027158efd517930479bc35601d17a71452369

  MIT, Copyright (c) 2026 Charles Bonetti / ASV Labs (LICENSE)

What was rewritten: the agent-side core. The original registered ten observer
hooks on Hermes and built a SQLite graph of files, URLs, queries, tool calls,
model calls, turns and agents, with receipts, a signing key, a re-fetching
verify and a desktop page. Here one observer on the :TOOL point of a Nodecode
tool call does the same work: it hashes what the call wrote (named by
`write-file`/`edit`, or found by walking the session's folder before and after
`sh`, `eval` and `patch`), records the files and URLs the call named as
observed, scans the artifact's own text for evidenced sources, and takes what
the session had seen earlier as inferred. The same three bases, the same
credential skip list, and a receipt JSON with the artifact's hash, versions,
producer and sources. `/file-lineage` and two model tools replace the original's
slash command and CLI.

What was left out, and why: the SQLite store is a JSON file under the cell's
home, since a cell has no database dependency; model-call, turn, agent and
search nodes are not recorded, because a Nodecode turn is not the same shape
as a Hermes one and the artifact's own receipt is what a reader asks for; the
HMAC install key and the sidecar signature check, the `verify --refetch`
network path, the Mermaid/Graphviz exporters' depth options and the desktop
page are not carried over. The sidecar is written, and its body digest is
verifiable, without a signature.
